eck: the write proof itself. If I write prgRom 0x6794 (the $70) to e.g. $6E, reset and start 1-1, and the Goomba's upper-left is drawn with a different tile, that proves the byte is used for the Goomba's metasprite. That's the required proof.\n\nBut to be thorough about \"the table\", let me bound it. The table = EnemyGraphicsTable. I can bound start and end by examining structure and possibly by finding references.\n\nLet me read from 0x6740 to 0x6860 and identify where entries stop making sense.\n\nActually, from known SMB disassembly, EnemyGraphicsTable is 6-byte entries and there are about 36 entries = 216 bytes? Let me recall. The table contains entries for: Goomba, Koopa (4 frames), Buzzy, Piranha, ... and Bowser? Actually Bowser uses a separate table.\n\nHmm. Let me just read a large chunk and find where the 6-byte entry pattern (lots of $FC) ends.\n\nLet me read 0x6740..0x6820 (224 bytes) - I already have 0x6740..0x679F. Let me read 0x67A0..0x6820.\n\nWait, I already have up to 0x67A0 from first read (0x6780+96 = 0x67E0). The first read at 26496=0x6780 length 96 gives 0x6780..0x67DF. Bytes:\n0x6780: DC DC DD DD DE DE FC FC B2 B3 B4 B5 FC FC B6 B3 B7 B5 FC FC 70 71 72 73 FC FC 6E 6E 6F 6F FC FC 6D 6D 6F 6F FC FC 6F 6F 6E 6E FC FC 6F 6F 6D 6D FC FC F4 F4 F5 F5 FC FC F4 F4 F5 F5 FC FC F5 F5 F4 F4 FC FC 00 ...\nLet me list from the earlier output:\n[0x6780] DC,DC,DD,DD,DE,DE,\nFC,FC,B2,B3,B4,B5,\nFC,FC,B6,B3,B7,B5,\nFC,FC,70,71,72,73,\nFC,FC,6E,6E,6F,6F,\nFC,FC,6D,6D,6F,6F,\nFC,FC,6F,6F,6E,6E,\nFC,FC,6F,6F,6D,6D,\nFC,FC,F4,F4,F5,F5,\nFC,FC,F4,F4,F5,F5,\nFC,FC,F5,F5,F4,F4,\nFC,FC,F5,F5,F4,F4,\nFC,FC,FC,FC,EF,EF,\nFC,FC,BD,BD,BC,BC,   <- wait let me recount\nThe output: \"220,220,221,221,222,222,252,252,178,179,180,181,252,252,182,179,183,181,252,252,112,113,114,115,252,252,110,110,111,111,252,252,109,109,111,111,252,252,111,111,110,110,252,252,111,111,109,109,252,252,244,244,245,245,252,252,244,244,245,245,252,252,245,245,244,244,252,252,245,245,244,244,252,252,252,252,239,239,185,184,187,186,188,188,252,252,189,189,188,188,122,123,218,219,216,216\"\n\nSo after the F5/F4 entries: \n252,252,252,252,239,239  -> FC FC FC FC EF EF (0x67C8)\n185,184,187,186,188,188 -> B9 B8 BB BA BC BC (0x67CE)\n252,252,189,189,188,188 -> FC FC BD BD BC BC (0x67D4)\n122,123,218,219,216,216 -> 7A 7B DA DB D8 D8 (0x67DA)\n\nSo table continues past 0x67DA. Let me read 0x67E0 onward.\n\nGiven the known SMB table continues with more entries, the table could end around 0x6800-ish. Let me read 0x67E0..0x6860.\n\nActually, better bounding approach: find the table's start and end via the code that indexes it. Since trace read on ROM didn't work, maybe I can find the pointer in code via search_memory for the table's start address bytes. If the table starts at, say, 0x67E0? Hmm.\n\nAlternatively, disassemble code that references it. Search prgRom for the immediate/pointer to the table. The code likely does something like:\nLDA EnemyGraphicsTable,Y ... but more likely it uses a pointer table of enemy graphic offsets.\n\nIn SMB, the routine is at $9FE1? Hmm. Let me search for byte patterns. If table starts at prgRom offset X, CPU = X+0x8000. Look for that 16-bit little-endian in PRG.\n\nBut I don't know start. However, the found entries around Goomba: the pattern of the known table. Let me recall the actual SMB EnemyGraphicsTable from disassembly:\n\n```\nEnemyGraphicsTable:\n      .db $fc, $fc, $70, $71, $72, $73 ; Goomba\n      .db $fc, $fc, $6e, $6e, $6f, $6f ; Green Koopa\n      .db $fc, $fc, $6d, $6d, $6f, $6f ; Red Koopa\n      .db $fc, $fc, $6f, $6f, $6e, $6e ; Green Koopa (mirrored)\n      .db $fc, $fc, $6f, $6f, $6d, $6d ; Red Koopa (mirrored)\n      .db $fc, $fc, $f4, $f4, $f5, $f5 ; Buzzy Beetle\n      .db $fc, $fc, $f4, $f4, $f5, $f5\n      .db $fc, $fc, $f5, $f5, $f4, $f4\n      .db $fc, $fc, $f5, $f5, $f4, $f4\n      .db $fc, $fc, $fc, $fc, $ef, $ef ; Piranha Plant\n      .db $fc, $fc, $bd, $bd, $bc, $bc ; Piranha Plant\n      .db $b9, $b8, $bb