{"type":"thinking","text":"$61 is a narrow tall shape, mostly columns; not great. $D6 is a filled rounded blob (flower head) — very high contrast: filled quadrants. $00 is a diagonal wedge.\n\n$D6: rows filled with 2/3/1, a big solid rounded block. Under palette 7 (slot2 peach, slot3 brown, slot1 black), it'd render a solid round blob of peach/brown/black. Very visible against the Goomba's dark outline.\n\nActually, even simpler and unmistakable: tile $D6 is a solid rounded mass filling most of the tile. Substituting $70 (a diagonal outline corner) with $D6 gives a solid blob in the Goomba's upper-left. Clearly different.\n\nBut hmm, the black background of the goomba area: palette7 slot1 = $0F black. $D6 has index1 pixels (black) and index2/3 (peach/brown). So it'd be a solid peach/brown blob. Very visible on the periwinkle sky.\n\nLet me go with $D6.\n\nWait, but is tile $D6 in the Goomba's CHR pattern table? The Goomba uses sprite pattern table 0 (list_sprites sprite_pattern_addr 0). Tiles are 0-255. $D6=214. Fire flower's tiles are $D6/$D9 in \"SPRITE PATTERN TABLE $0000\" per mapping. So yes, $D6 is in the same pattern table. \n\nHmm, but the Goomba is drawn with tile $70 etc. from the same table. Setting $70→$D6 means the table entry for the Goomba's upper-left now points at the flower graphic. The renderer will draw tile $D6 at that OAM slot. Should work.\n\nNow use_hack eval-sheet2-d.\n\nThen write_memory prgRom 0x6794 = 214 ($D6) with reason.\n\nWait — careful about which byte. The Goomba entry: 0x6792=FC, 0x6793=FC, 0x6794=70, 0x6795=71, 0x6796=72, 0x6797=73. So 0x6794 is the $70. Change to $D6.\n\nThen reset_emulator, start 1-1 (press Start, wait, press Start), get Goomba on screen, screenshot.\n\nNote: reset_emulator also clears palette overrides, and the ledgered ROM edits survive reset (they're in the emulator's ROM copy). But careful: after reset, does the emulator reload the ROM from disk? The tool description says a ROM edit still visible after reset is persistent in the emulator's copy. So the write persists across reset.\n\nLet me use_hack now.\n\nThen write.\n\nLet me also think about where to place Mario to see the Goomba. After reset, we're at title. Press Start twice to enter 1-1. Then the Goomba is at level x~290, offscreen initially. Hold right ~200 frames to bring it on screen, then pause and screenshot. As before.\n\nActually, when I started 1-1 earlier with play_input [{right,200}] the Goomba was at screen x ~122 with Mario possibly dead. To get a clean screenshot with the Goomba visible and not dying, I can pause as soon as the Goomba is on screen. Let me: after entering 1-1, play_input [{right,200}] then pause_emulator, screenshot, list_sprites.\n\nHmm, but if Mario dies, the level reloads and the Goomba resets. Let me just do right 180 and pause.\n\nActually, a cleaner way to show the Goomba: the first Goomba walks left toward Mario. If I just stand still, the Goomba reaches Mario in a few seconds and the collision happens. Alternatively, I can write Mario's position to bring the Goomba on screen. But simplest: hold right for ~200 frames then pause.\n\nWait, in my earlier run holding right 200, the Goomba was at screen x 122 and no Mario sprite (Mario likely died from the Goomba). Hmm, but the Goomba was on screen. Good enough for a screenshot. But better to have Mario visible too for context.\n\nLet me do: enter 1-1, then play_input [{right,120}] (Mario moves toward the Goomba), pause, screenshot; check Goomba visible. If not, adjust.\n\nActually another approach: place Mario near the Goomba using RAM writes (mapping knows player_x $006D/$0086, camera). Overkill.\n\nLet me just do it and look.\n\nNow, the ledgered write. Let me call use_hack first."}
